From ca8e9eb361914d4868f27308fda74d18ccc7ee2d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 17:02:46 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] fix(tools): process wait gets its own 400 s window,
 descriptions stop recommending sleep [daveey]

Measured on the hub over 7 days: 52 h of agent time in 2,838 terminal calls
shaped like `sleep 540; tail run.log`; half waited on a background process the
same session had started. The tools steered agents there:
- process_manage(action="wait") was clamped to TERMINAL_TIMEOUT (180 s default,
  60 s in the hub's .env), shorter than a foreground sleep.
- The terminal description said "To wait a fixed time, run the wait in the
  foreground".

Changes:
- tools/process_wait_limit.py: the wait's own limit, TERMINAL_MAX_WAIT, default
  the larger of 400 s and TERMINAL_TIMEOUT, kept 20 s under the executor's
  per-call deadline (420 s) so the wait ends by itself before the executor
  abandons the call. ProcessRegistry.wait and foreign_wait both use it; an
  omitted timeout waits the full window (the call still returns the moment the
  process exits).
- Terminal and process_manage descriptions point at the wait instead of a
  foreground sleep; prompt-surface snapshot re-rendered (+50 tool tokens).
- TERMINAL_MAX_WAIT documented in .env.example and the env-var reference.

test_process_wait_clarity: the clamp test now sets TERMINAL_MAX_WAIT, since
TERMINAL_TIMEOUT no longer clamps the wait, and also asserts the clamp note.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
